A||||12||huggy||Lock Cylinders||||5||
Z||000000||huggy||09-25-2000||11:33 AM||||Has anyone had any luck with setting door lock cylinders to match keys? ||||reg||5
Z||000001||StuGood||09-25-2000||11:40 AM||||Yes, I have.  Why?||||reg||2
Z||000002||HugGRRRFiero||09-25-2000||11:48 AM||||I replaced a lock cylinder on mine, it's a little time consuming but not too hard to do.<P>I used the same "tumblers" from my old bunged up one so the key would work.  Had a heck of a time getting them out of the old cylinder.  Keep them in order and make sure you even note what end of the "tumbler" drops in first.  I ordered a new lock cylinder from a Pontiac dealer, cleaned up the tumblers (used about 600 grit sandpaper to clean the corrosion off) and dropped them into the new cylinder.<P>I don't know what you could do to make the tumblers work for a specific key tho...  I guess you could take the lock cylinder from the other door that the key works for, tear it apart and see if you can get enough lock cylinders from the boneyard to match up the "tumblers" for it to work.<P>------------------<BR>          <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/huggrrr.gif">        <BR>        <A HREF="mailto:HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com">HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||huggy||09-25-2000||11:50 AM||||StuGood, Can you give me a brief discription on how to do it.<p>[This message has been edited by huggy (edited 09-25-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||huggy||09-25-2000||11:57 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by HugGRRRFiero:<BR><B>I replaced a lock cylinder on mine, it's a little time consuming but not too hard to do.<P>I used the same "tumblers" from my old bunged up one so the key would work.  Had a heck of a time getting them out of the old cylinder.  Keep them in order and make sure you even note what end of the "tumbler" drops in first.  I ordered a new lock cylinder from a Pontiac dealer, cleaned up the tumblers (used about 600 grit sandpaper to clean the corrosion off) and dropped them into the new cylinder.<P>I don't know what you could do to make the tumblers work for a specific key tho...  I guess you could take the lock cylinder from the other door that the key works for, tear it apart and see if you can get enough lock cylinders from the boneyard to match up the "tumblers" for it to work.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE> Thanks I will give it a try. Also thanks for your response on the horn<BR>button it now works.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||StuGood||09-25-2000||12:16 PM||||Oh, c'mon - since when are <B>any</B> of my descriptions brief??  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>First of all, I'd call a locksmith, and get an estimate.  Honest.  Tell him/her exactly what you want done, and that you'll bring in the cylinder(s) and key.  You may find that the $ they want is perfectly reasonable, versus what your time is worth, especially if they're only rekeying one cylinder.<P>(This message has been brought to you by the National Association of Locksmiths. J/K  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> )<P>If you want to rekey more than one cylinder (like two doors+ trunk lock), you might pull three matching cylinders from a salvaged car (assuming it's got the key with).  Check to make sure the key works smoothly in all (3?) locks.<P>When you get the cylinders + key home, pull out the barrels (the rotating part) and swap them with the barrels of your existing cylinders.  Might have to paint the ends black to match the ones on your car.  <P>If you really want to rekey your existing lock cylinder, e-mail me.  This "short description" is getting too long already.        ||||reg||12
Z||000006||lawrence||09-25-2000||12:30 PM||||Re-coding locks is very straight forward, especially if you have the old lock to re-use the tumblers.  When you take them apart, be careful because the springs will go sprong and you may loose the tumblers.<P>Don't worry if you loose the sequence, because you can put the key into the housing and see if the tumblers are where they belong.  I re-coded my 88 about a year after I got it, and just recently re-coded my Buick.  They are different locks, but they operate in a similar fashion.<P>I have an explanation of what I did in my magazine (link below).  Go to the Archives and look for the Door Key issue.<P>Good luck,<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://www.webelectricmagazine.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.webelectricmagazine.com</A>  - WebElectric Magazine||||reg||1
Z||000007||HugGRRRFiero||09-25-2000||12:38 PM||||Cool, glad I could help!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Beep Beep....  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif"> ||||reg||14
Z||000008||StuGood||09-25-2000||12:42 PM||||Thank goodness!  Someone's already written it up!  That'll spare huggy from having to read one of my cobbled-together, windy discourses.<P>By the way, lawrence, what's this "Dorky issue?"  Sounds like something Stu needs to read   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> !  Is my picture in it?  <p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 09-26-2000).]||||reg||12
Z||000009||pollock||09-25-2000||01:24 PM||||or take it to a locksmith and pay $10.||||reg||1
Z||000010||webbee||09-25-2000||09:35 PM||||I'd go with the locksmith every time. It's cheap and he has new pins. Why reuse a disposable part?||||reg||1
Z||000011||HugGRRRFiero||09-26-2000||08:55 AM||||I don't know...  maybe to save $10?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"><P>Make sure you rotate your lock cylinders from one door to the other every 10,000 miles so you get a longer life out of them.  Imagine the money you will save in the long run.  I take mine in and get new ones every 50,000 miles.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000012||Seanh||09-26-2000||07:37 PM||||I got new ones from my Pontiac dealer here and they come with the new knockdown pins. Just thought I would let ya know.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
